    icon: '≡', name: 'Item Master', featureKey: null,
    guide: 'Your ingredient database. Every item needs a name, category, UOM (unit of measure), and one price: what ONE unit costs. Items are always stored in their smallest unit — sauce per ML, salt per GM — because that is what recipe costing needs. If you only know what a whole pack cost, the "Bought a pack?" line does the division for you: type 500 GM for NPR 388.50 and the price fills in as 0.777 per GM. Wherever a price per GM or per ML is shown — Item Master, purchase bills, returns, Price Tracker, Recipe Costing, Requisitions, the stock reports — it reads per KG or per litre first, with the stored figure beside it: NPR 777.00 per KG (0.777 per GM). You still type it per GM, and Excel exports keep the per-GM figure so a spreadsheet can multiply it by grams. Set conversion factors if you buy in bulk packs but consume in smaller units (e.g. 1 CTN = 24 BTL).',
    tips: ['Check allergens no longer calls a food Not food just because its name holds a packaging word such as bowl: when a food word like sauce, gravy or marination comes after it, the row asks you to check the label.', 'The short USDA list in the nutrition library was checked against FoodData Central: wrong figures now match it, and foods it does not carry (beaten rice, paneer, khuwa, jaggery, river fish) fill from the Nepal or Indian table instead.', 'Import Items: download the item template, fill one item per row with the same boxes as Add Item, then import it. A name already in Item Master is skipped and listed afterwards, and nothing already on file is changed. A category named in the file that does not exist yet is created', 'Manage categories: add your own categories or rename them. To remove one, pick where its items and monthly budgets go; only the owner or an inventory manager can remove. Sub-Recipes is kept by Recipe Costing and cannot be renamed or removed. A category from head office is changed at head office', 'Once an item has purchases, counts or recipe lines, its unit can no longer be changed — every past quantity was recorded in that unit. Hide the item and create a new one instead. Changing an item\'s price asks first and says how many past counts and entries it re-values', 'Use consistent UOMs — GM for solids, ML for liquids', 'Two ways to price an item, same result: type the per-unit price straight in, or use "Bought a pack?" (500 GM for NPR 388.50) and let Crest divide it down', 'Both "Bought a pack?" boxes accept arithmetic — type "12*4" for four dozen and it commits 48, with the running total previewed above the box (Enter applies, Esc cancels)', 'The "Bought a pack?" boxes are a calculator, not saved data — they are blank every time you reopen an item. If a pack size is a permanent fact about the item, put it on the Conversion tab instead, which is also what tells the Purchase Bill whether its Qty column means packs or grams', 'The Rate (NPR) / UOM column is the price of ONE unit — the exact figure recipe costing and stock valuation use. A sub-paisa rate is normal for something bought by the thousand and shows extra decimals rather than a misleading 0.00', 'Set conversion factor if 1 case = 24 bottles etc.', 'The unit chips beside the category chips (GM, ML, PCS and so on, each with its count) narrow the list to items counted in one unit. They combine with the category, search and Used In filters, and a printed list names the unit it was filtered to', 'Deactivate items you no longer use — do not delete them. An item with any history behind it cannot be deleted anyway, and hiding it keeps every record it is already on: past months keep its purchases and counts on every report. Hiding only takes it off the pickers and the stock count sheet from now on, so hide it once its stock is at zero', 'Every new item gets its own item code, even when you add several in a row. Recipe Import matches ingredients by code first, so if two older items ever ended up sharing one code, the import refuses that code and names both items rather than guessing', 'The Used In column names where an item already has records — Purchases, Recipes, Opening or Closing Stock, Wastage, Staff Meals, Stock Transfers and so on. It shows the first two, then +N; hover the chip for the full list. An item showing any of them cannot be deleted. A stock count of 0 counts too: "we counted and there was none" is still a record. The Unused filter lists exactly the items with no records, which are the ones Del can remove. To delete an item whose only record is a 0 count this month, clear its cell on Stock Count first (a blank cell removes the count, a 0 keeps it)','Each item name is used once per outlet. If a name is already taken — including by a sub-recipe, which is stock-counted alongside your items — the save is refused and tells you which row has it, because two rows with one name split that ingredient\'s purchases and stock between them', 'Editing many items in a row? The Edit Item dialog has ← Prev / Next → buttons that save the current item and jump straight to the next one in the list (in the order shown), with an "X of Y" counter — no need to close and reopen. (Same on the Vendors edit dialog.)', 'Item Master shows your item list straight away. The Used In column says checking… for a moment while Crest looks up where each item is used, and until that check finishes, Delete, the unit box and the Used In filters wait, so nothing is deleted or changed on a guess', 'With the Nutrition Facts add-on, each item has a Nutrition button beside Edit: the same nutrition and allergens form the recipe screen opens. The Allergens column shows what each ingredient contains, or △ Not checked, and the "△ Allergens not checked" toggle lists the ones still to do. Saving the form marks the ingredient checked, even with nothing ticked', 'Check allergens… (beside that toggle) lists every unchecked ingredient with Crest\u2019s guess from its name and the word each allergen came from: Milk (paneer), Gluten (aata), Not food (cup). Confirm a row that is right, or Confirm all shown; open the rest with Change…. A sauce, a spice mix or another mixed product says Check the label and is never confirmed on a guess', 'A name that looks misspelt, such as ICE CREAN, is marked Check the label with nothing ticked and says what it looks like (cream), so Confirm all never saves it as allergen-free. Fix the spelling or tick its allergens yourself', 'Cups, lids, packaging and cleaning supplies can be marked Not food, in the Nutrition form or the Check allergens list: they then never count as unchecked or as missing nutrition data in a recipe']
