    guide: 'Record every ingredient purchase here. Each bill has a header (vendor, day, invoice ref, discount, payment method) and one row per item. Per row: select item, enter qty and rate (leave the rate at 0 for free goods — buy 10 get 1 free), or type the line Total exactly as printed on the bill and let Crest work the rate back out, tick VAT if this line attracts 13% VAT, optionally enter an expiry date or shelf-life days (expiry auto-fills from the bill date). Above the lines, say how the bill prints its line totals: After VAT when each line already includes VAT, Before VAT when VAT is added once at the bottom (supermarket tax invoices usually print this way). Pick the wrong one and every VAT line is 13% off. A PAN bill (from a supplier not registered for VAT) has no VAT on any line: leave every VAT box unticked, and the choice disappears because each Total is used exactly as printed. Fill in the VAT and invoice total "as printed on the supplier\'s bill" and Crest tells you before saving if they do not match. Use the Returns tab to log items sent back to suppliers, on the day they went back.',
    tips: ['If your outlet gives PAN bills (not VAT-registered), the 13% VAT on a supplier\'s VAT bill cannot be claimed back, so Crest counts it as food cost: Monthly Summary, the P&L, the dashboards and Item Master\'s price all include it, while the VAT and Non-VAT Reports and what you owe suppliers are unchanged. Each bill keeps the basis it was saved on, so switching VAT registration later changes only new bills. Confirm this treatment with your accountant', 'After you save a NEW bill, Crest offers to update the Item Master price for any item whose price on the bill is different. Nothing is ticked — tick only the items whose price really changed. It is offered only to a supervisor, manager or the owner, only for a new bill in the open month, and not for an item that already has a later bill this month: changing the Item Master price re-values every past stock count and recipe cost, so an old or edited bill never offers to roll it back', 'If you left changes to a bill unsaved and someone else saved that same bill afterwards, Crest does not quietly put your old version back. It opens the bill as it is saved now and asks: use your unsaved version, or keep the saved bill', 'A bill you have not saved yet is kept safe if the page closes on you. Switch to another app, let Chrome update itself, or leave the tab in the background long enough for the tablet to drop it — reopen Purchases and what you had typed comes back, with an amber bar saying so. On a shared tablet it comes back only for the login that typed it. Nothing is recorded until you press Save; use "Start a blank bill" on that bar if you would rather begin again', 'If Crest cannot read your business name, a notice at the top of the bill says the voucher will not print. The bill still saves. To get the voucher, reload the page before saving — what you typed comes back', 'If an item on a bill you have not saved yet was hidden in Item Master in the meantime, Crest will not save that line: it can no longer tell whether the Qty means cartons or single units. Remove the row, or make the item active again in Item Master',
      'Entering a bill off paper is faster from the keyboard: "+ Add Item" drops the cursor into the new line, and pressing Enter in the Days box at the end of a row starts the next line for you', 'Goods sent back after the month they were bought are recorded in the month they went back: on the Returns tab pick the bill\'s month under "Bill from". Which month the VAT on such a return is claimed in is a question for your accountant — confirm it with them before filing. You can also type the VAT and total exactly as printed on the supplier\'s bill; Crest flags any difference over NPR 1 but never stops a save', 'Each bill shows the time it was ENTERED into Crest under the vendor name — not when the goods arrived. The Day column is the delivery day; where the two differ, the entry date is shown next to the time, so a bill filed late is obvious', 'Enter purchases daily from the actual invoice — not from memory at month end', 'Always enter the actual invoice rate if it differs from the master rate', 'A row with an item but no quantity is refused by name, never silently skipped — fill it in or remove it with ×. A rate of 0 is a free line: stock goes up, spend does not', 'If the same vendor\'s bill number is already on record, Crest asks before saving it again — say Save anyway only if the vendor genuinely reused the number', 'A bill with vendor payments recorded against it (in Outstanding Payables) cannot be edited or deleted — remove the payments first. Otherwise money already paid would vanish from Payment Report', 'Delete All (the whole month) is Supervisor and above; Staff can still add, edit and delete single bills','Add invoice reference number for audit trail — the Bill no. box above the table then finds that bill instantly, matching any part of the reference (the tail digits are usually enough) with or without the leading #', 'Filter by Payment to see what you still owe: pick Credit for the unpaid bills, and the footer totals and entry count follow the filter. The list only offers methods this period actually used', 'VAT is per-line — tick only the items that are VAT-able on that invoice. If you typed the line Total, ticking or unticking VAT keeps that Total and changes the Rate instead: Qty 10 and Total 1,130 with VAT ticked gives Rate 100 plus 13% VAT, whichever you did first. If you typed the Rate, ticking VAT adds 13% on top of it and the Amount goes up. The VAT switch in the bill header works the same way on every line', 'On an outlet whose supplier VAT is part of the cost (PAN bills), a Rate that Item Master filled in moves with the VAT tick until you type over it, so ticking VAT never adds the 13% twice', 'Enter shelf-life days and the expiry date fills automatically from the bill date', 'Returns auto-inherit rate and vendor from the original purchase', 'Deleting or editing a return that had settled a bill (paid down to what the return left, or closed with "Close this bill") — including moving it onto a different bill — sends that bill back to Outstanding Payables if it now owes money again, and Crest asks first, naming how much. Because it reopens a settled bill, it needs an IMS manager, the Owner or your Crest admin — any other login is told why and nothing changes. On a very old bill, paid before Crest recorded payments, check the amount the confirmation names: if the supplier was in fact already paid, cancel — or go ahead and then record that old payment on Outstanding Payables so the bill closes again', 'Very small rates keep their decimals on the register, the Returns tab and the printed voucher: cheese bought by the gram at NPR 0.004 shows 0.004, and 0.115 shows 0.115 — never a rounded 0.00','Daily Register tab: one row per item with a column per day plus a Total column at the end summing that item\'s purchases across the whole period — also included in Export Excel', 'The Total column stays pinned to the right edge as you scroll — no need to scroll all the way right to see it', 'Click any category header in Daily Register to collapse/expand it and cut down scrolling on a long item list', 'On a phone the bill list is one card per bill: the supplier and the bill total on the first line, then the bill number, item count and payment method, with the items one tap away and Edit and Delete under each bill. The total payable is at the foot of the list']
